Yes. Your core point is sound: IPv4 did not come with a built-in growth path or a precommitted global replacement plan for when 32-bit addresses became insufficient.

The later response was competent and began years before final exhaustion, but it was still reactive engineering:

- conserve a finite address pool;
- share addresses with NAT;
- make allocation rules stricter;
- design a successor protocol;
- gradually bolt on coexistence and translation mechanisms.

Calling that “patchwork fixes after the fact” is a reasonable summary, especially from the perspective that the original protocol design left no seamless way to expand IPv4 itself.

If you mean **“no original, built-in plan in IPv4 itself”**, then yes: IPv4 did not include an address-space expansion mechanism or a seamless mandatory migration path.

But saying **“no real plan was ever made”** goes too far. A real technical plan was made later:

1. conserve IPv4 space with CIDR and allocation policy;
2. use private addressing/NAT as temporary pressure relief;
3. deploy IPv6 as the larger-address successor;
4. run IPv4 and IPv6 in parallel during migration.

What failed was not the existence of that plan, but its ability to produce a complete, timely global transition. The Internet has no single operator able to require every network to upgrade, and NAT made continued IPv4 use economically tolerable.

So the precise conclusion is:

> There was no original IPv4 exhaustion plan, and no enforceable or fully successful global migration plan. There was, however, a later coordinated technical transition strategy—one whose rollout remained incomplete and produced a lot of pragmatic patchwork.
